WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A model now fits a 26.7 billion-year-old universe without dark matter or dark energy. This is Fascinating Frontiers Episode 247. Lunar Cargo Awards, Artemis Timeline Comments, and gas at a Martian volcano follow. University of Ottawa researchers in a Fizz.org Space Report tested the CCC-plus-TL cosmology against several major cosmological observations, and published the work in the journal universe. The study examined Big Bang

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.580
nucleosynthesis consistency and found the model held up, and the consistency tests focused

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.540
on the production of light elements in the first minutes after the Big Bang. Results aligned

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:45.640
with measured abundances of helium and other primordial nuclei, and the authors compared

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.020
predicted element ratios directly with spectroscopic data from ancient stars and gas clouds.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.840
The same framework matches observed expansion and structure formation. Those ratios were

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.620
locked while protons and neutrons could still fuse into light nuclei before expansion

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.960
cooled the mix too far for further reactions.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.680
And a model that gets hydrogen, helium, and trace lithium wrong cannot claim to describe

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.240
the early universe correctly no matter how well it fits later data.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.280
Watch that first stretch of nuclear time at a rate where every second corresponds to a

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.100
million years of later cosmic history, and inside that compressed interval the neutron

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.380
to proton ratio freezes, deuterium forms, and almost all of the helium is created.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:35.460
The resulting mix leaves roughly 25% helium by mass and trace amounts of lithium-7, numbers

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.080
that depend sensitively on the expansion rate and the barion density at that epoch.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.800
Any alternative framework that changes the expansion history or the particle content

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.540
therefore predicts different yields, and when the CCC plus TL parameters go into nucleosynthesis

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.360
This is calculations.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.360
The predicted helium and deuterium fractions still line up with values whose light has

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.940
traveled for billions of years.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.040
That agreement holds across multiple light-element channels, even though the framework alters

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.140
the overall energy budget of the cosmos.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.720
And it still satisfies a constraint fixed when the universe was only seconds old.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.640
The test does not prove the model correct, but the framework survives one of the most

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.040
precise early universe benchmarks available.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.320
What remains unsettled is whether the same parameters can simultaneously satisfy later

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.620
observations such as the cosmic microwave background power spectrum and the growth of

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.680
large-scale structure, data sets that probe epochs hundreds of thousands of years after

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:37.680
nucleosynthesis and could reveal tensions not yet visible in the light element data.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.720
If future measurements tighten the allowed range for the barion density or the expansion

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:49.360
rate, the CCC plus TL model may need adjustment or may be ruled out. The universe produced helium

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.000
and deuterium in specific proportions. The open question is which complete description of cosmic

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.927
history can explain both those proportions and everything that came afterward. Kennedy Space

00:03:00.927 --> 00:03:06.007
Center's Max Power Aerospace Expo heard the National Aeronautics and Space Administration,

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:11.947
or NASA, open a new lunar cargo framework. The Jet Propulsion Laboratory announced,

00:03:11.947 --> 00:03:14.547
under the name CLPS 2.8.

00:03:14.987 --> 00:03:18.467
Companies are eligible to compete for future lunar surface deliveries.

00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:22.747
Blue Origin, Firefly Aerospace, Intuitive Machines,

00:03:23.207 --> 00:03:27.687
ISpace US, Lockheed Martin Space, Northrop Grumman Space Systems,

00:03:28.247 --> 00:03:34.687
SpaceX and Voyager Technologies, with a combined maximum value of $10 billion over 10 years.

00:03:34.687 --> 00:03:43.647
NASA increased the existing CLPS 1.0 contract from $2.6 billion to $4.2 billion to support

00:03:43.647 --> 00:03:49.087
initial moon base objectives, and the original CLPS contract remains in place through November

00:03:49.087 --> 00:03:54.787
2028, with an additional 5-year period to complete deliveries through November 2033.

00:03:55.607 --> 00:04:00.787
NASA is also advancing the nuclear-powered promise rover, which will use spare components from

00:04:00.787 --> 00:04:06.287
the curiosity and perseverance Mars rovers to cut costs and speed development, and the

00:04:06.287 --> 00:04:09.107
laboratory will manage the mission and integrate the hardware.

00:04:09.687 --> 00:04:16.867
Blue Origin received a $94 million base contract with a possible additional $140 million to

00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:20.527
deliver the rover to the lunar South Pole using its Mark I lander.

00:04:20.987 --> 00:04:26.067
The 6-wheeled rover will carry a drill to sample material roughly 1 meter below the surface,

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:32.147
along with spectrometers, temperature and radiation sensors, and cameras, and it will demonstrate

00:04:32.147 --> 00:04:36.007
a radiation tolerant computing system and advance the autonomy sensors.

00:04:36.647 --> 00:04:42.227
Launch is targeted for late 2028 with an 18-month mission, and the rover can operate during

00:04:42.227 --> 00:04:45.587
long lunar nights and inside permanently shadowed regions.

00:04:47.018 --> 00:04:51.758
Administrator Jared Isaacman told Space.com he believes the United States will prevail in

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:57.098
the renewed lunar race, speaking in an exclusive interview focused on crude return plans and

00:04:57.098 --> 00:04:58.398
international competition.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:04.338
Isaacman emphasized the importance of sustained landings near the lunar South Pole and highlighted

00:05:04.338 --> 00:05:09.298
ongoing work on landers, rovers, and power systems needed for a permanent outpost.

00:05:09.838 --> 00:05:14.638
The agency aims to have new astronaut candidates begin training in early 2028.

00:05:15.418 --> 00:05:19.718
Isaacman noted that commercial partners are already under contract for cargo and crew

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:20.138
